Amara writes:
   Kennita also says:
   >BTW -- I talked to my gynecologist this week, and he said that part of
   >the reason that eggs aren't frozen more often is that they would need
   >to be ripened first, then collected one by one; it doesn't work well
   >to take them directly out of the ovary while they're still "green",
   >because we don't know what's involved in the maturing them inside the
   >ovary.  

   [BTW, Thanks, Kennita, for the info.]
   The solution to this seems simple to me.  Freeze the egg(s) in their
   'unripe' stage, and when the woman is ready to have them fertilized,
   put them back in her ovary where she can 'ripen' them.  

Unfortunately, we don't know how the ovary picks one which of the
hundreds of thousands of eggs to ripen in any given month.  (Another
point of information that I didn't know before:  eggs are not ripened 
selectively on the side of the ovary facing the Fallopian tube.  They
bubble out at some random point on the surface, and many of them end
up floating off into the abdominal cavity.)  Maybe when we get control
over this process, egg freezing/reimplantation will be more feasible.
Also, don't forget the freezing damage.  Ova are the largest cells in
the human body, and as a consequence I'd expect they'd get the most
messed up by water expansion.

I think it'll be a while before all this is licked, but in the
meantime you may want to see if you can take a fertility drug and have
a few eggs collected and frozen for a time when we get it figured out.
You'd probably have to do it with ready money, though -- I don't think
insurance would cover it.

