Date: Mon, 27 Dec 1993 14:02:03 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: "Epigenetic"

According to Webster's (via Han Huang, thanks):

> epi.gen.e.sis \.ep-*-'jen-*-s*s\ n [NL] 1: development involving gradual
>diversification and differentiation of an initially undifferentiated entity
>(as a spore) 2: change in the mineral character of a rock owing to outside
>influences

My usage came from E.O. Wilson, _On Human Nature_.  He apparently
either recoined "epigenetic" as a special technical term, or derived
his meaning from definition (1) and his observation that genetic influence 
dominates cell differentation.  However (2) stresses outside influences,
albeit in another domain.  I suggest we avoid the term "epigenetic"
and stick to "environmental" or "memetic" vs. "genetic".

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

