Date: Mon, 27 Dec 1993 14:33:30 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Why have children?

Nancy Lebovitz: 
> However, preferring to have sons only makes sense for high-status
> people in societies where males inherit.

Inheritance itself is often driven more by parental emotion
than societal tradition.  I suspect these biases are not
a straight economic calculation, but more driven by genetic
"economics" via instinctually derived emotion.  In most
societies rich sons tend to have much more "womb access" than poor 
sons, while there's little difference for rich and poor daughters.

For the selfish gene it makes sense for rich hosts to favor
sons and poor hosts to favor daughters.  If "rich" and "poor" is 
triggered by some signal that indicated abundant resources
in a hunter-gatherer environment, then there would be a bias 
for modern folks to favor sons.  As usual with instincts these are 
emotionally/unconsciously driven, not consciously plotted rational 
decisions.  These biases won't change unless they're genetically
engineered out (difficult, since genes usually perform multiple
functions) or consciously worked around (which may also be hard --
try working around the instincts of eating and sleeping).
Again, cf. Symon's _The Evolution of Human Sexuality_ for 
an excellent discussion of this.

