It depends whether we're talking genetic therapy or just sperm donors.
The "monster risk" from Nobel sperm isn't any greater than from
average sperm.  One group of people who benefit from early genetic 
therapy are those with genetic defects.  For these folks the "monster 
risk" from having the therapy is less than from avoiding it.  Fixing 
defects usually involves just one or a few genetic loci, thus this kind of 
engineering is much easier to do and more reliable than enhancement. 
After we gain quite a bit of experience with that, and experience
with enhancement of animals and non-germ-line enhancement of adult
humans, Extropian folks will feel more comfortable to experiment 
with human germ-line enhancement.

