Subject: Speed of evolution/selection of traits in humans...

Carl Feynman responds to me:

\In a lot of cultures, signing up with the military was a great way to get
/ahead.  In feudal Europe, the basic activity of the upper classes was
\leading gangs into hand-to-hand combat.  If you could put together a fairly
/big gang, and carve out a territory, you could declare yourself a Baron, or
\Duke, or even King.  Then you could father lots of kids on the peasant
/women, and be an evolutionary success.  I sometimes wonder if this accounts
\for the height and brawniness of modern western Europeans.  Because of this
/ability to rise in the ranks by violence, feudal Europe was a culture in
\which uppitiness was sometimes highly rewarded, thus preserving uppity
/genes for future generations.

It all depends on the historical period, I guess; another advantage
of being in the military (if I may argue with myself) would simply be
increaced mobility. You might get to travel more than twenty miles
from your birthplace; if you are monogamous, your genes may have a
better chance of propagating than everyone else's because you probably
won't be marrying a relative.

I'm not sure that modern western Europeans are taller than everyone
else, though. Some close friends of mine from grade school were a
Filipino family who had immigrated (one step ahead of Marcos' police
coming to arrest them on trumped up weapons charges) when the eldest
child was three.

Both parents were about 5 feet tall. All three children are somewhere
around 6 foot 2 inches. I strongly suspect diet had a lot to do with
it.

\Promotion to the upper classes promotes the accumulation of biomass.  In
/many cultures, a man in the upper classes will leave twice as many
\descendants in the next generation as someone in the lowest class.  Of
/course, this means that half of the kids of the upper classes have to be
\kicked out into the lower classes every generation, or else the upper
/classes would soon overwhelm society.  I'm not suggesting that there is a
\gene for "being upper class"; such a gene is clearly not very prevalent,
/since most people are peasants.  But in a meritocratic society with a
\strong variation of reproduction with status, genes that promote moving up
/will rapidly accumulate more individuals.

Actually, the upper classes of many societies have been set up so that
massive reproduction is limited (thus keeping dispersal of wealth/power
to a minimum) or limiting "upper-class" status to the firstborn. Lots
of the second and third etc. children in aristocracies would end up in
monasteries/nunneries or the army, where there might or might not be 
(that's the other argument, remember) an increaced favorability of
genetic broadcasting.

I'm also not sure that bastards had a very good chance of broadcasting
genes in feudal society, although this too depends on period, etc.
YMMV.

